CREATE DATABASE atm_db;
USE atm_db;

CREATE TABLE accounts (
    account_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    account_holder VARCHAR(50),
    balance DECIMAL(10,2)
);

INSERT INTO accounts VALUES
(101, 'Aarav', 10000),
(102, 'Priya', 5000),
(103, 'Rahul', 8000);

SELECT * FROM accounts;

START TRANSACTION;

SELECT * FROM accounts
WHERE account_id = 101;

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance - 2000
WHERE account_id = 101
AND balance >= 2000;

COMMIT;

SELECT * FROM accounts;

START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance - 15000
WHERE account_id = 102
AND balance >= 15000;

ROLLBACK;

SELECT * FROM accounts;
A distributed transaction involves multiple systems participating in a single logical transaction. In an ATM withdrawal, the system verifies the account and balance, deducts money, and dispenses cash. COMMIT saves the transaction if it succeeds, whereas ROLLBACK cancels the changes if a failure occurs. Transaction recovery helps restore the database after a crash and prevents incorrect balances or incomplete transactions. In a real distributed banking system, transaction coordination may use protocols such as Two-Phase Commit.

